    "continuity": "If met: AT-03 — reaction and start to changing signals. If not: return to foundation version, reduce distance or load, and model again.",
    "bankLink": "Tags: athletics, sprinting, running technique, acceleration, school PE.\nSport: Athletics. Age: 10–12. Equipment: cones, floor marks, batons, soft balls, mats, tape.\nSkills: running posture, arm action, stride rhythm, progressive acceleration, peer observation.\nLoad: moderate. Complexity: intermediate.\nKeywords: tall posture, forward–back arm action, stride rhythm, 20-metre acceleration, build-up run, relaxed speed, run-through finish.",
    "systemData": "Physical: moderate. Cognitive: moderate. Organizational: moderate. Independence: medium.",
    "teacherSummary": "State goal and cues.\nWarm up gradually.\nBuild technique without pressure.\nAdd decision and challenge.\nClose with evidence and goal.",
    "pedagogicalValue": "Why important? Running is the most common movement in physical education and the least often taught; a tall posture and an efficient arm action make every student faster and less tired without any gain in fitness.\nWhat develops? Postural control at speed, a forward–back arm action that sets the leg rhythm, the patience to let stride length build rather than reaching for it, and the ability to observe technique in a partner.\nWhy not skip? Students who never learn running mechanics simply strain harder, which tightens the shoulders, shortens the stride, and persuades slower students that speed is fixed at birth rather than something they can improve.",
