    "bankLink": "Tags: athletics, reaction time, sprint start, signal response, school PE.\nSport: Athletics. Age: 10–12. Equipment: cones, floor marks, batons, soft balls, mats, tape.\nSkills: reaction to a cue, stillness in the set position, response inhibition, directional choice, balanced acceleration.\nLoad: moderate. Complexity: intermediate.\nKeywords: reaction time, false start, set position, visual cue, sound cue, anticipation, directional start, personal target.",
    "systemData": "Physical: moderate. Cognitive: moderate. Organizational: moderate. Independence: medium.",
    "teacherSummary": "State goal and cues.\nWarm up gradually.\nBuild technique without pressure.\nAdd decision and challenge.\nClose with evidence and goal.",
    "pedagogicalValue": "Why important? Reaction is the element of sprinting that transfers most directly into every game the students play — responding to a ball, an opponent, or a whistle — and unlike top speed it improves quickly with practice.\nWhat develops? The stillness that makes a genuine reaction possible, perception of a cue under time pressure, inhibition of the anticipated action, and balanced acceleration from unfamiliar body positions.\nWhy not skip? Students never taught to wait learn instead to guess, which produces false starts in athletics and mistimed movements in every invasion game; guessing also succeeds often enough to become permanent unless it is addressed directly.",
