    "priorKnowledge": "AT-03 — reaction and start.",
    "pathwayPosition": "Previous lesson: AT-03 — reaction and start.\nCurrent lesson: It turns the individual sprinting of AT-01 to AT-03 into a shared task: two runners must now match speeds, communicate, and move a baton between them inside a marked zone. It withholds competitive timing entirely — exchanges are scored for safety and accuracy, never for how fast a team finishes — and it withholds the endurance pacing AT-05 introduces.\nNext lesson: AT-05 — personal endurance pacing.",
    "unitContribution": "Motor: coordination, posture, power, endurance. Social: turns and feedback. Cognitive: regulation and decisions. Tactical: pace and space planning. Emotional: persistence and confidence.",
    "equipment": "Four lanes and stations; cones, floor marks, batons, soft balls, mats, and tape as needed. Setup 6 minutes; pack-down 4.",
    "safety": [
      "Check surface, equipment, and footwear.",
      "Keep fixed lanes and travel direction.",
      "Start only on signal and with clear space.",
      "Collect equipment after a full stop.",
      "Keep two arm lengths.",
      "Match load to ability; stop for pain or dizziness.",
      "No public comparison or forced pace."
    ],
    "sections": {
      "opening": "3 minutes. Teacher says: “The baton travels faster than any of us, so nobody should ever slow down to hand it over. Hold your hand still, listen for the call, and keep running forwards.” Organisation: class in a half-circle beside one marked exchange zone. Purpose: state the cues — call, stable hand, pass forward, continue in your lane — plus the rule that nobody turns around to look for the baton.",
      "warmup": "7 minutes. Purpose: prepare shoulders and legs for running while reaching behind, and rehearse the hand position before any baton moves at speed. Structure: individual movement, then pairs walking. Instructions: jog and change direction on a colour call; add arm swings and leg swings; then walk in pairs, one behind the other, practising the receiving hand held still behind the hip while the partner places the baton into it. Teaching points: the receiving hand is a stationary target, not something that reaches back and grabs. Transitions: move to lanes on the signal. Variations: foundation — stationary exchange from standing; advanced — exchange at a jog.",
      "mainA": "10 minutes. Purpose: build the exchange pattern at a speed where nothing can go wrong. Procedure: Pairs complete exchanges inside a marked zone, first walking and then jogging, the incoming runner calling and the outgoing runner holding a still hand. Work 40 seconds, then 20 seconds of paired feedback, repeated four times. Organisation: four lanes, one pair active per lane, exchange zone marked at both ends, walking return outside the lanes. Teaching points: the outgoing runner looks forward, never back; the receiving hand stays open and still behind the hip; the incoming runner places the baton rather than throwing it; both keep moving forwards through the exchange. Common errors: turning the head to look for the baton; the receiving hand waving or reaching; the incoming runner slowing to make the pass easier; the exchange happening outside the marked zone.",
      "mainB": "10 minutes. Purpose: solve the real problem of a relay — that two runners travelling at different speeds must briefly travel at the same one. Procedure: On a straight lane the outgoing runner starts when the incoming runner reaches a marked check point, and the pair adjusts that mark until the exchange happens comfortably in the middle of the zone. Roles change every minute. Organisation: pairs per lane with a movable check-point cone they reposition themselves; an observer records where the exchange actually occurred. Teaching points: if the exchange happens early, move the check mark closer; if it happens late or outside the zone, move it further away; the outgoing runner accelerates properly rather than jogging and waiting. Common errors: the outgoing runner starting too late and being caught immediately; starting too early and running away from the baton; never adjusting the mark after a failed exchange; both runners watching each other instead of looking forward.",
      "appliedGame": "10 minutes. Purpose: run a genuine relay in which the reward is a clean exchange rather than a fast finish. Rules: Cooperative relay in teams of four; a team earns a point for every exchange completed inside the zone with a still hand, a clear call, and no turning of the head. A dropped baton is simply picked up and the relay continues. No times are recorded and no team is declared the winner. Scoring: quality and decision points only; no individual ranking. Teaching points: a team with four clean exchanges has done the lesson, in whatever order they finish; call loudly enough to be heard over the other lanes. Common errors: speed creeping up until exchanges fall apart; the call disappearing once it starts to feel like a race; runners stopping immediately after handing over, in the path of the next lane.",
      "closing": "5 minutes. Key message: “A relay is not four sprints — it is three exchanges with running in between, and the exchanges are what you actually win or lose.” Learning summary: each student states one piece of evidence about their hand position or call, plus their next personal goal. Feedback: partners exchange one observed fact and one suggestion. Orderly exit: walk to lower the pulse, collect batons and cones by zone, then a head count."
    },
