    "bankLink": "Tags: athletics, relay, baton exchange, cooperation, school PE.\nSport: Athletics. Age: 10–13. Equipment: cones, floor marks, batons, soft balls, mats, tape.\nSkills: baton exchange, receiving hand position, speed matching, timing a start, communication.\nLoad: moderate. Complexity: intermediate.\nKeywords: exchange zone, still hand, check mark, speed matching, call and pass, cooperative relay, no look-back.",
    "systemData": "Physical: moderate. Cognitive: moderate. Organizational: moderate. Independence: medium.",
    "teacherSummary": "State goal and cues.\nWarm up gradually.\nBuild technique without pressure.\nAdd decision and challenge.\nClose with evidence and goal.",
    "pedagogicalValue": "Why important? The relay is the only genuinely cooperative event in athletics, and the exchange rather than the running is what decides it — it is also where students who are not the fastest discover they can be the most valuable.\nWhat develops? Matching speed with another person, a stable receiving hand while moving, timing a start from a visual check point, and the trust required to run forward while something arrives from behind.\nWhy not skip? Untaught exchanges mean students turn around, stop, or snatch at the baton, which is slow and is also the most common cause of collisions and falls in school relays.",
