    "bankLink": "Tags: athletics, endurance, pacing, effort regulation, school PE.\nSport: Athletics. Age: 11–14. Equipment: cones, floor marks, batons, soft balls, mats, tape.\nSkills: pacing, effort rating, talk test, rhythmic breathing, self-regulation, lap splits.\nLoad: moderate. Complexity: intermediate.\nKeywords: personal pace, talk test, effort scale, even splits, negative pacing, keeping a reserve, self-competition.",
    "systemData": "Physical: moderate. Cognitive: moderate. Organizational: moderate. Independence: medium.",
    "teacherSummary": "State goal and cues.\nWarm up gradually.\nBuild technique without pressure.\nAdd decision and challenge.\nClose with evidence and goal.",
    "pedagogicalValue": "Why important? Pacing is the difference between endurance running becoming a lifelong activity and remaining a punishment; it is also the single factor that most determines whether a student decides they are “bad at running”.\nWhat develops? The ability to read one's own effort and breathing accurately, the self-discipline to start slower than the group, honest self-rating, and the experience of finishing a run strongly rather than merely surviving it.\nWhy not skip? Students never taught to pace set off with the fastest child in the class, fade within two minutes, and conclude they lack fitness; the real deficit is a pacing strategy, and it can be taught in a single lesson.",
    "selfQualityCheck": "✅ Fully original\n✅ Safety checked\n✅ Observable goal\n✅ Age appropriate\n✅ Realistic equipment\n✅ Learning pathway\n✅ Adaptations\n✅ Assessment\n✅ Reflection\n✅ Field-ready\nStatus: Original V2 author draft; professional, language, and pilot review required before publication.",
