    "adaptations": "Foundation: short distance, walking pace, wide target. Intermediate: full pace and one decision. Advanced: time, precision, or complex sequence. Large group: six lanes. Small: pairs. Limited space: short stations. Partial equipment: share in pairs. Varied needs: visual mark, extra time, fixed partner, adapted range.",
    "assessment": "Checks: technique, control, regulation, safety, independence. Success: 4–5 criteria. Partial: 2–3 with prompt. Not yet: 0–1 or unsafe; return to foundation version.",
    "reflection": "Teacher: Who acted independently? Was load appropriate? Students: What evidence shows improvement and what is next? Next: revisit one criterion.",
    "continuity": "If met: AT-07 — approach run and jump to zone. If not: return to foundation version, reduce distance or load, and model again.",
    "bankLink": "Tags: athletics, jumping, standing long jump, landing mechanics, school PE.\nSport: Athletics. Age: 10–12. Equipment: cones, floor marks, batons, soft balls, mats, tape.\nSkills: two-footed take-off, arm swing, controlled landing, force regulation, distance judgement.\nLoad: moderate. Complexity: intermediate.\nKeywords: standing long jump, arm swing, two-foot take-off, soft landing, freeze the landing, personal marker, landing zone.",
    "systemData": "Physical: moderate. Cognitive: moderate. Organizational: moderate. Independence: medium.",
    "teacherSummary": "State goal and cues.\nWarm up gradually.\nBuild technique without pressure.\nAdd decision and challenge.\nClose with evidence and goal.",
    "pedagogicalValue": "Why important? The two-footed take-off and landing underpins every jumping and rebounding action in sport, and a stiff, uncontrolled landing is a recognised knee-injury risk when it is never taught.\nWhat develops? Coordinated arm and leg drive, a symmetrical two-footed take-off, eccentric strength through the landing, and the body awareness to hold a position still rather than simply arrive in it.\nWhy not skip? Students jump constantly in every other sport, and if a controlled landing has never been taught they absorb it with straight legs; teaching it here, standing and unhurried, is far easier than correcting it later at speed.",
    "selfQualityCheck": "✅ Fully original\n✅ Safety checked\n✅ Observable goal\n✅ Age appropriate\n✅ Realistic equipment\n✅ Learning pathway\n✅ Adaptations\n✅ Assessment\n✅ Reflection\n✅ Field-ready\nStatus: Original V2 author draft; professional, language, and pilot review required before publication.",
