    "sections": {
      "opening": "3 minutes. Teacher says: “The run-up is the jump. If your last three steps are even and your eyes stay up, the take-off happens by itself — if you look down to find the line, you stop jumping and start stepping.” Organisation: class in a half-circle beside one approach lane and landing zone. Purpose: state the cues — even rhythm, eyes forward, firm take-off, soft landing — plus the rule that only one jumper approaches at a time.",
      "warmup": "7 minutes. Purpose: prepare the legs for a one-footed take-off at speed and rehearse the approach rhythm without jumping. Structure: individual movement, then approach walk-throughs. Instructions: jog and change direction on a colour call; add ankle bounces, leg swings, and two build-up runs; then walk and jog the 5–7 stride approach repeatedly without taking off, counting the strides aloud. Teaching points: count the approach so it becomes repeatable; the last two steps are quick, not long. Transitions: move to approach lanes on the signal. Variations: foundation — three-stride approach at walking pace; advanced — seven strides at running pace.",
      "mainA": "10 minutes. Purpose: establish a repeatable approach before any jump is attempted, since that is what makes the take-off consistent. Procedure: Students run their 5–7 stride approach and step over the take-off mark without jumping, repeating until the same foot lands on the mark every time and adjusting the start point until it does. Work 40 seconds, then 20 seconds of paired feedback, repeated four times. Organisation: four approach lanes, one runner at a time, a partner watching where the take-off foot actually lands and moving the start cone accordingly. Teaching points: adjust the start of the run, never the last stride; count the strides every time; run through the mark rather than at it; eyes on a target ahead, not on the ground. Common errors: stuttering or chopping the last steps to hit the mark; stretching for the mark and losing speed; changing the count between runs; looking down and losing posture.",
      "mainB": "10 minutes. Purpose: add the jump now the approach is reliable, with a wide zone so distance never pressures the landing. Procedure: Students run the rehearsed approach, take off from one foot at the mark, and land on two feet inside a wide marked zone or mat, holding the landing. Roles change every minute. Organisation: two approach lanes onto mats or a soft zone, a landing judge at the side, and a clear waiting line; only one jumper in the air at a time. Teaching points: drive the free knee up at take-off; keep the chest up rather than diving forward; bring both feet together before landing; bend the knees to absorb. Common errors: landing on one foot; over-running the mark because the approach was rushed; leaning forward and landing on the hands; the next jumper starting before the zone is clear.",
      "appliedGame": "10 minutes. Purpose: finish with accuracy of take-off, rather than distance, as the thing being measured. Rules: Each student jumps in their own lane, scoring points for taking off within the marked take-off area, for an even approach rhythm, and for a held two-footed landing. A long jump that took off before or beyond the area scores nothing. No distances are compared between students. Scoring: quality and decision points only; no individual ranking. Teaching points: an accurate take-off is worth more here than a big jump, because in athletics a foul jump does not count at all; keep the same approach every time. Common errors: lengthening the approach to gain distance and missing the mark; abandoning the stride count under pressure; landing poorly while reaching for extra distance.",
      "closing": "5 minutes. Key message: “The jump is decided before you leave the ground — same run-up, same count, eyes up, and the take-off looks after itself.” Learning summary: each student states one piece of evidence about their approach rhythm or landing, plus their next personal goal. Feedback: partners exchange one observed fact and one suggestion. Orderly exit: walk to lower the pulse, collect mats and cones by zone, then a head count."
    },
