    "continuity": "If met: AT-08 — soft-ball distance throw. If not: return to foundation version, reduce distance or load, and model again.",
    "bankLink": "Tags: athletics, jumping, approach run, take-off, school PE.\nSport: Athletics. Age: 11–14. Equipment: cones, floor marks, batons, soft balls, mats, tape.\nSkills: approach rhythm, stride counting, one-footed take-off, two-footed landing, take-off accuracy.\nLoad: moderate. Complexity: intermediate.\nKeywords: approach run, stride count, take-off mark, free knee drive, landing zone, foul jump, eyes forward.",
    "systemData": "Physical: moderate. Cognitive: moderate. Organizational: moderate. Independence: medium.",
    "teacherSummary": "State goal and cues.\nWarm up gradually.\nBuild technique without pressure.\nAdd decision and challenge.\nClose with evidence and goal.",
    "pedagogicalValue": "Why important? It joins the two halves of jumping — running speed and a controlled take-off — and teaches that consistency of approach, not effort at the board, is what produces a good jump.\nWhat develops? A repeatable stride pattern under acceleration, converting horizontal speed into height, one-footed take-off strength, and a two-footed landing that stays safe even when arriving fast.\nWhy not skip? Students jumping from a free run-up stutter, foul, or take off from the wrong foot, which is ineffective and increases the risk of a twisted ankle at take-off; a counted approach addresses all three at once.",
