    "continuity": "If met: AT-09 — combined athletics stations. If not: return to foundation version, reduce distance or load, and model again.",
    "bankLink": "Tags: athletics, throwing, force transfer, sector safety, school PE.\nSport: Athletics. Age: 11–14. Equipment: cones, floor marks, batons, soft balls, mats, tape.\nSkills: side-on throwing stance, weight transfer, release angle, force sequencing, sector safety routine.\nLoad: moderate. Complexity: intermediate.\nKeywords: side-on stance, weight transfer, high elbow, release angle, throwing sector, clear signal, distance zones.",
    "systemData": "Physical: moderate. Cognitive: moderate. Organizational: moderate. Independence: medium.",
    "teacherSummary": "State goal and cues.\nWarm up gradually.\nBuild technique without pressure.\nAdd decision and challenge.\nClose with evidence and goal.",
    "pedagogicalValue": "Why important? Throwing for distance is the clearest demonstration in physical education that force travels from the ground upwards through the body, and it is the event where technique most visibly outperforms raw strength.\nWhat develops? A side-on throwing stance, sequential force transfer from legs through trunk to arm, judgement of release angle, and the disciplined safety routine every throwing event requires.\nWhy not skip? Untaught, students throw flat and entirely with the arm, producing short distances, sore shoulders, and the belief that throwing is simply a matter of strength; the safety routine is also the one part of athletics where an untaught class is genuinely dangerous.",
